Tap to wake: while iOS has the display off, a double tap presses the power key (user request: "I had it on android").
iOS's display off only darkens the backlight here (0242, 1137): the panel and its TDDI touch controller (NT36672A /
FT8719) keep scanning, so touches keep arriving. Now, while off (sweet_display_off, set on RequestPowerChange):
 - touches no longer reach iOS (fingers iOS saw down are released first);
 - one finger down < 300 ms and moved < 80 px is a tap; a second tap within 400 ms and 150 px of the first presses
   the power key (50 ms), which wakes the phone like the side button; taps are ignored for 1.5 s after that.
boot-arg and sysctl kern.sweet_tap2wake: 1 double tap (default), 2 single tap (iOS's own Tap to Wake style), 0 off.
No proximity sensor yet: a double tap in a pocket wakes the screen.

